1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,968
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:01,968 --> 00:00:04,428
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:04,428 --> 00:00:06,888
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:10,608 --> 00:00:11,400
TOBIAS COLDING: OK.

5
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:17,200
So today, we want to talk a
little bit more about sequences

6
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:25,080
and how sequences connect to
a function being continuous.

7
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:27,080
And then the second
thing we will talk about

8
00:00:27,080 --> 00:00:29,800
is metric spaces.

9
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:38,640
So let me just remind you first
that if you take a subset here,

10
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:45,400
say, of R and you have
a function A into R,

11
00:00:45,400 --> 00:01:09,810
then f is said to be
continuous if for all x0 in A,

12
00:01:09,810 --> 00:01:23,130
the function is
continuous at x0.

13
00:01:23,130 --> 00:01:49,690
And the function
is continuous at x0

14
00:01:49,690 --> 00:01:56,250
if for all epsilon
greater than 0,

15
00:01:56,250 --> 00:02:11,930
there exists delta greater than
0 such that if the two points--

16
00:02:11,930 --> 00:02:19,830
so x0 and this other point, x,
they deviate at most by delta.

17
00:02:19,830 --> 00:02:23,840
Then their images deviate
at most by epsilon.

18
00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:34,390
And in general, this delta here
depends not only on x0 but also

19
00:02:34,390 --> 00:02:36,450
on epsilon.

20
00:02:36,450 --> 00:02:40,270
So delta would in
general be expected

21
00:02:40,270 --> 00:02:42,600
to depend on both
of these things.

22
00:02:45,210 --> 00:02:47,750
So we talked about
this a number of times.

23
00:02:47,750 --> 00:02:51,950
And we will also generalize
this later in the lecture.

24
00:02:51,950 --> 00:02:54,950
So this is what it means to
be continuous, that it needs

25
00:02:54,950 --> 00:02:57,100
to be continuous at all points.

26
00:02:57,100 --> 00:03:03,360
And this is what it means
to be continuous at a point.

27
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:06,400
Now, two things we
talked about before

28
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:20,130
is the extreme value theorem and
the intermediate value theorem.

29
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:32,600
And I want to think
about these two theorems

30
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:34,140
in terms of sequences.

31
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:42,240
And that's often a really
useful way of thinking about it.

32
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:46,140
So the extreme value theorem
is that we have a function--

33
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:52,160
so the extreme value theorem,
abbreviated like this,

34
00:03:52,160 --> 00:03:56,700
that's the theorem that says
that if you take a function

35
00:03:56,700 --> 00:04:00,060
on some interval from a to b--

36
00:04:00,060 --> 00:04:04,980
a and b are both
included into R.

37
00:04:04,980 --> 00:04:07,040
And this function is continuous.

38
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:14,790
Then the claim is that
then there exist--

39
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:28,300
so one way of formulating it
is that then there exists xM--

40
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:32,400
capital M. This is
something in that interval.

41
00:04:32,400 --> 00:04:37,860
So there exists some points
that we call x capital M--

42
00:04:37,860 --> 00:04:43,380
and x little m also
in the interval.

43
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:45,500
And the first one
has the property

44
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:56,150
that for all x in
the interval, you

45
00:04:56,150 --> 00:05:04,310
have that f of x is less than
or equal to the value at xM.

46
00:05:04,310 --> 00:05:05,830
So you have this.

47
00:05:05,830 --> 00:05:10,030
And you also have that
f of x, that this here

48
00:05:10,030 --> 00:05:16,270
is bigger or equal to the
value at this other point.

49
00:05:16,270 --> 00:05:21,310
So this means in particular
that the maximum and the minimum

50
00:05:21,310 --> 00:05:23,310
is achieved.

51
00:05:23,310 --> 00:05:25,550
It also means that the
function is actually

52
00:05:25,550 --> 00:05:27,750
bounded on that interval.

53
00:05:27,750 --> 00:05:29,660
So that's an extreme
value theorem.

54
00:05:32,630 --> 00:05:34,270
And we talked about
that before, but I

55
00:05:34,270 --> 00:05:36,690
want to think about it
in terms of sequences.

56
00:05:36,690 --> 00:05:38,670
And the other thing
I want to think

57
00:05:38,670 --> 00:05:45,990
about in terms of sequences is
the intermediate value theorem.

58
00:05:45,990 --> 00:05:53,370
So the intermediate value
theorem say that all values--

59
00:05:53,370 --> 00:05:56,490
let me just
formulate it loosely.

60
00:05:56,490 --> 00:06:03,610
All values between-- sorry.

61
00:06:03,610 --> 00:06:05,360
f here is as above.

62
00:06:08,850 --> 00:06:14,090
So you have, again, a
function from the integral,

63
00:06:14,090 --> 00:06:19,430
like this, into R. And
this is continuous.

64
00:06:23,410 --> 00:06:26,850
So you have this
continuous function.

65
00:06:26,850 --> 00:06:30,530
And then the intermediate value
theorem-- so loosely formulated

66
00:06:30,530 --> 00:06:46,860
is, say that all values between
f of a and f of b are achieved.

67
00:06:51,460 --> 00:06:54,920
So this means that if
you take a function--

68
00:06:54,920 --> 00:06:56,980
so you have a function.

69
00:06:56,980 --> 00:07:01,460
This here is the
value at the endpoint.

70
00:07:01,460 --> 00:07:04,420
Here, you have the
value in the beginning.

71
00:07:04,420 --> 00:07:09,080
Then it say that everything
in between these two--

72
00:07:09,080 --> 00:07:14,420
these here-- is the
image of something.

73
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:18,820
That's what the intermediate
value theorem say.

74
00:07:18,820 --> 00:07:19,320
OK.

75
00:07:19,320 --> 00:07:24,900
And I want to prove these
two using sequences.

76
00:07:24,900 --> 00:07:31,540
And so in order to do that,
we need the following lemma,

77
00:07:31,540 --> 00:07:34,220
which is often--

78
00:07:34,220 --> 00:07:38,040
so, again, this here is like the
definition of being continuous.

79
00:07:38,040 --> 00:07:43,820
But often, when you're proving
that something is continuous,

80
00:07:43,820 --> 00:07:47,220
you would instead
use this lemma here.

81
00:07:47,220 --> 00:07:50,700
So it's really an if
and only if statement,

82
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:54,460
but I will just prove
the if statement.

83
00:07:54,460 --> 00:07:56,380
So if f is continuous--

84
00:08:03,840 --> 00:08:07,480
so it's only the if statement
I need in this case here.

85
00:08:07,480 --> 00:08:11,800
But in other cases, it's
useful to have the if and only

86
00:08:11,800 --> 00:08:12,840
if statement.

87
00:08:12,840 --> 00:08:14,720
So if f is continuous--

88
00:08:25,660 --> 00:08:29,120
let me just say where
the function is defined.

89
00:08:29,120 --> 00:08:37,799
So in this case, it's just
defined on some subset of r.

90
00:08:37,799 --> 00:08:44,680
If this here is
continuous and xn

91
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:51,690
is a sequence in a and
xn converge to x and x

92
00:08:51,690 --> 00:09:01,730
is also in a, then the claim
is that then the sequence here

93
00:09:01,730 --> 00:09:07,810
converge to this.

94
00:09:07,810 --> 00:09:13,530
And remember that if
this here is the case,

95
00:09:13,530 --> 00:09:19,050
then we write this here
as x is the limit of xn

96
00:09:19,050 --> 00:09:21,610
as n goes to infinity.

97
00:09:21,610 --> 00:09:24,210
This statement
here can be written

98
00:09:24,210 --> 00:09:33,970
as that f of x is the limit as
n goes to infinity of this thing

99
00:09:33,970 --> 00:09:35,930
here.

100
00:09:35,930 --> 00:09:38,730
And so if you combine
these two things,

101
00:09:38,730 --> 00:09:42,130
if you fill in x
is equal to that,

102
00:09:42,130 --> 00:09:44,610
then this here can
also be written

103
00:09:44,610 --> 00:09:52,870
as that f of the limit of
xn as n goes to infinity--

104
00:09:52,870 --> 00:09:55,910
this is this by
filling in this--

105
00:09:55,910 --> 00:09:58,570
and then equal to the limit.

106
00:09:58,570 --> 00:10:04,230
So in a way, if you
want to think about it

107
00:10:04,230 --> 00:10:06,710
like it's something about--
that if it's continuous,

108
00:10:06,710 --> 00:10:11,010
you can exchange the
order of taking limits.

109
00:10:11,010 --> 00:10:13,530
OK.

110
00:10:13,530 --> 00:10:14,030
Yeah.

111
00:10:14,030 --> 00:10:16,170
But that's a more
formal way, in a way.

112
00:10:16,170 --> 00:10:19,930
But I was just thinking
about it like this.

113
00:10:19,930 --> 00:10:20,430
OK.

114
00:10:20,430 --> 00:10:23,770
So we want to prove this lemma.

115
00:10:23,770 --> 00:10:26,110
And so then we want
to use this lemma

116
00:10:26,110 --> 00:10:27,540
to prove these two theorems.

117
00:10:31,670 --> 00:10:32,170
OK.

118
00:10:32,170 --> 00:10:35,400
So now let's prove this lemma.

119
00:10:45,640 --> 00:10:56,440
So we want to show that f
of xn converge to f of x.

120
00:10:56,440 --> 00:10:58,780
So I'm first going
to use that f.

121
00:10:58,780 --> 00:11:15,600
So use first that the f is
continuous at this point, x.

122
00:11:15,600 --> 00:11:19,560
So this is some point in a.

123
00:11:19,560 --> 00:11:29,920
So this means that, therefore,
given epsilon greater than 0,

124
00:11:29,920 --> 00:11:40,600
there exist delta
greater than 0 such that.

125
00:11:40,600 --> 00:11:48,780
If x minus-- so any
other element, y,

126
00:11:48,780 --> 00:11:52,780
if this thing here
is less than delta,

127
00:11:52,780 --> 00:12:02,220
then their images
deviate at most epsilon.

128
00:12:02,220 --> 00:12:04,580
This is just using
the definition

129
00:12:04,580 --> 00:12:09,060
that x here is now thought
about as being some fixed

130
00:12:09,060 --> 00:12:10,600
point, a fixed point.

131
00:12:10,600 --> 00:12:13,840
And then y is like
any other point.

132
00:12:13,840 --> 00:12:14,340
OK.

133
00:12:14,340 --> 00:12:17,300
So we have that.

134
00:12:17,300 --> 00:12:29,460
But then, second, we use that
the sequence xn converges to x.

135
00:12:29,460 --> 00:12:33,300
So this means that now
thinking about this delta

136
00:12:33,300 --> 00:12:36,820
here that this epsilon
is spitting out--

137
00:12:36,820 --> 00:12:49,850
so this means that for delta,
there exist capital N such that

138
00:12:49,850 --> 00:12:56,230
if little n is bigger
than the capital N,

139
00:12:56,230 --> 00:13:03,718
then actually, xn minus
x is smaller than delta.

140
00:13:07,030 --> 00:13:09,430
But you see that-- so
now combining this--

141
00:13:17,270 --> 00:13:19,510
so maybe I'll go over here.

142
00:13:19,510 --> 00:13:34,910
So combining this, then
we get that if n here

143
00:13:34,910 --> 00:13:41,010
is bigger than capital
N, then xn minus x

144
00:13:41,010 --> 00:13:43,690
is smaller than delta.

145
00:13:43,690 --> 00:13:48,030
But this means that now if
they are smaller than delta,

146
00:13:48,030 --> 00:13:51,410
then the first part,
say, that their images--

147
00:13:51,410 --> 00:13:59,530
so this means that f of xn minus
f of x is smaller than epsilon.

148
00:13:59,530 --> 00:14:01,130
And that's exactly
what we wanted

149
00:14:01,130 --> 00:14:06,450
to prove to show that if you
are sufficiently far out, then

150
00:14:06,450 --> 00:14:09,490
these two things are
very close together.

151
00:14:09,490 --> 00:14:13,330
So that was quite easy.

152
00:14:13,330 --> 00:14:15,450
Now I want to use
these-- but this

153
00:14:15,450 --> 00:14:21,630
is like you use this sort
of thing for continuity.

154
00:14:21,630 --> 00:14:24,370
This is a standard thing to use.

155
00:14:24,370 --> 00:14:26,190
It's used implicitly.

156
00:14:26,190 --> 00:14:29,730
And again, this
one has a converse.

157
00:14:29,730 --> 00:14:31,540
And the converse is
also very useful.

158
00:14:34,170 --> 00:14:39,100
So now let's come back
to the extreme value

159
00:14:39,100 --> 00:14:41,370
theorem and the
intermediate value theorem.

160
00:14:47,780 --> 00:14:50,140
So now let's try to prove--

161
00:14:50,140 --> 00:15:09,540
so using the lemma, we'll
show the extreme value

162
00:15:09,540 --> 00:15:16,080
theorem and the
intermediate value theorem.

163
00:15:16,080 --> 00:15:20,980
And so now first, to prove
the extreme value theorem--

164
00:15:20,980 --> 00:15:26,070
so proof of the
extreme value theorem.

165
00:15:29,540 --> 00:15:33,300
I'm first going to
prove that the image--

166
00:15:33,300 --> 00:15:47,850
so first show that the image of
this interval here is bounded.

167
00:15:51,520 --> 00:15:54,400
So this is what I
first want to show.

168
00:15:54,400 --> 00:15:56,360
And so I suppose not--

169
00:15:56,360 --> 00:15:57,310
so suppose not.

170
00:16:02,520 --> 00:16:15,160
This means that for each
n which is an integer,

171
00:16:15,160 --> 00:16:17,360
let's say that it's
not bounded from above.

172
00:16:17,360 --> 00:16:20,700
To show that it's not bounded
from below is similar.

173
00:16:20,700 --> 00:16:25,360
So I'm just going to prove
that it's bounded from above.

174
00:16:25,360 --> 00:16:31,960
But to prove that it
is bounded from below

175
00:16:31,960 --> 00:16:35,810
is similar with
obvious modifications.

176
00:16:35,810 --> 00:16:38,970
So suppose it's not
bounded from above.

177
00:16:38,970 --> 00:16:43,210
Then for each
integer, there must

178
00:16:43,210 --> 00:16:57,890
exist x in this interval
such that f of x

179
00:16:57,890 --> 00:16:59,630
is bigger or equal to n.

180
00:16:59,630 --> 00:17:02,250
Because otherwise, n would
have been an upper bound.

181
00:17:02,250 --> 00:17:11,170
And so I just pick
such a n-- so pick.

182
00:17:11,170 --> 00:17:18,310
such n, x-- and
let's call it xn.

183
00:17:18,310 --> 00:17:21,530
So for each n, you now have xn.

184
00:17:21,530 --> 00:17:27,569
And it has a property that f
of xn is bigger or equal to n.

185
00:17:32,230 --> 00:17:36,690
Now you have that this
gives you a sequence.

186
00:17:36,690 --> 00:17:40,390
So you have that xn
here is a sequence.

187
00:17:40,390 --> 00:17:47,030
It's a sequence in-- sorry,
it's in this interval here.

188
00:17:47,030 --> 00:17:50,790
But this means that it's
a bounded sequence--

189
00:17:50,790 --> 00:18:17,670
so a bounded sequence so that by
the Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem,

190
00:18:17,670 --> 00:18:30,390
it has a convergent
subsequence xnk.

191
00:18:33,120 --> 00:18:35,460
So you have that xnk--

192
00:18:35,460 --> 00:18:41,040
so this is this subsequence,
and it converges to some x.

193
00:18:44,340 --> 00:18:48,640
But now this means that
by the lemma over there,

194
00:18:48,640 --> 00:18:52,680
you have that this
sequence then,

195
00:18:52,680 --> 00:18:56,760
because the function was
continuous, converge to this.

196
00:18:56,760 --> 00:18:59,880
But on the other
hand, this here,

197
00:18:59,880 --> 00:19:09,800
xnk, is a subsequence of xn.

198
00:19:09,800 --> 00:19:12,300
But this means that
this number here--

199
00:19:12,300 --> 00:19:16,820
remember that a subsequence
just means that this here--

200
00:19:16,820 --> 00:19:18,760
it means in particular
that this here

201
00:19:18,760 --> 00:19:21,240
would be further out than n.

202
00:19:21,240 --> 00:19:26,280
So you have that f
of xnk is supposed

203
00:19:26,280 --> 00:19:28,640
to be bigger or equal to nk.

204
00:19:28,640 --> 00:19:32,260
But this is supposed
to be bigger than n.

205
00:19:32,260 --> 00:19:37,780
So these guys here,
these here goes to--

206
00:19:37,780 --> 00:19:42,180
you know these
here are unbounded.

207
00:19:42,180 --> 00:19:44,860
But on the other hand, we're
saying that this sequence

208
00:19:44,860 --> 00:19:46,660
converges to this sequence.

209
00:19:46,660 --> 00:19:49,700
So any convergent
sequence is bounded.

210
00:19:49,700 --> 00:19:51,140
So that's a contradiction.

211
00:19:56,386 --> 00:19:56,886
OK.

212
00:19:56,886 --> 00:20:01,780
So this is like proving
first using the lemma,

213
00:20:01,780 --> 00:20:08,340
we have now that the image
here is bounded from above.

214
00:20:08,340 --> 00:20:12,820
The almost exact same proof
with obvious modification

215
00:20:12,820 --> 00:20:15,340
give that it's
bounded from below.

216
00:20:15,340 --> 00:20:18,140
So now you have that
the image of this

217
00:20:18,140 --> 00:20:20,690
is actually bounded from
both above and below.

218
00:20:28,990 --> 00:20:33,530
So now this means that if you're
looking at the image here--

219
00:20:39,310 --> 00:20:41,170
this here is a bounded subset.

220
00:20:41,170 --> 00:20:43,200
So this is a bounded subset.

221
00:20:48,670 --> 00:20:52,560
And so you can look
at the sup of the set.

222
00:21:00,230 --> 00:21:04,470
You can look at the
sup of this set.

223
00:21:04,470 --> 00:21:06,630
And I'm going to take the sup--

224
00:21:06,630 --> 00:21:11,630
I'm going to call
this capital M.

225
00:21:11,630 --> 00:21:20,040
And now for each little
n-- the n is an integer.

226
00:21:22,910 --> 00:21:29,520
Then I can look at M minus 1/n.

227
00:21:29,520 --> 00:21:33,040
So this is obviously strictly
smaller than capital M.

228
00:21:33,040 --> 00:21:35,780
So it's not an upper bound.

229
00:21:35,780 --> 00:21:39,800
So this means that for
little n, this here is not

230
00:21:39,800 --> 00:21:42,240
an upper bound for this set.

231
00:21:42,240 --> 00:21:45,040
So this means that
there must exist

232
00:21:45,040 --> 00:21:57,612
an x so that you have this for
some x in this interval here.

233
00:21:57,612 --> 00:22:01,000
Because this here is
not an upper bound,

234
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:05,080
which means that there must
be something larger than that.

235
00:22:07,800 --> 00:22:12,280
So for each little n,
you just pick such x.

236
00:22:12,280 --> 00:22:15,280
And we call this xn.

237
00:22:15,280 --> 00:22:18,880
So xn is in the interval.

238
00:22:18,880 --> 00:22:26,020
And we have that m minus 1 over
little n is strictly less than f

239
00:22:26,020 --> 00:22:27,660
of xn.

240
00:22:27,660 --> 00:22:31,900
Remember that this thing here
is, of course obviously less

241
00:22:31,900 --> 00:22:34,040
than the sup over all images.

242
00:22:34,040 --> 00:22:35,200
So you even have that.

243
00:22:38,020 --> 00:22:40,220
OK.

244
00:22:40,220 --> 00:22:43,330
But now you see that now
you have a sequence here.

245
00:22:46,860 --> 00:22:50,540
So now you have a sequence.

246
00:22:50,540 --> 00:22:55,260
And so you have a sequence, xn.

247
00:22:55,260 --> 00:23:14,340
And so by the
Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem,

248
00:23:14,340 --> 00:23:19,040
this sequence here has a
