427
00:39:39,386 --> 00:39:43,030
So that's the proof of
the extreme value theorem

428
00:39:43,030 --> 00:39:44,870
and the intermediate
value theorem

429
00:39:44,870 --> 00:39:50,790
using sequences and using
that little lemma from before.

430
00:39:50,790 --> 00:39:51,930
OK.

431
00:39:51,930 --> 00:39:54,150
So now the next thing
I want to talk about

432
00:39:54,150 --> 00:39:57,550
is another really
important concept.

433
00:39:57,550 --> 00:40:00,970
And that, to some extent,
is a generalization

434
00:40:00,970 --> 00:40:04,560
of things we know
from the real numbers.

435
00:40:08,130 --> 00:40:10,760
And so this is this
concept of a metric space.

436
00:40:17,010 --> 00:40:19,430
And so let me just
give you a definition,

437
00:40:19,430 --> 00:40:23,050
and then I'll give you
a bunch of examples.

438
00:40:23,050 --> 00:40:29,450
So a metric space is a
set, X. So this is the set.

439
00:40:29,450 --> 00:40:36,250
And then it's a map for
each pair of points.

440
00:40:36,250 --> 00:40:39,250
So let me explain
what I mean by this.

441
00:40:39,250 --> 00:40:51,330
So whenever you have x in X and
another element in this space,

442
00:40:51,330 --> 00:40:54,980
then when you have
any such pair,

443
00:40:54,980 --> 00:41:00,960
then d x comma y, this
gives you a real number.

444
00:41:04,420 --> 00:41:07,700
And so this here is
what you usually call

445
00:41:07,700 --> 00:41:19,020
the distance between x and y.

446
00:41:19,020 --> 00:41:21,580
That's the distance
between x and y.

447
00:41:21,580 --> 00:41:24,340
Now, what properties
should this have?

448
00:41:24,340 --> 00:41:32,380
It's not just any map from this
space across itself into R.

449
00:41:32,380 --> 00:41:34,740
It has three properties.

450
00:41:34,740 --> 00:41:43,700
The first is that the distance
between pair of points--

451
00:41:43,700 --> 00:41:46,380
and then there are
generalization of this.

452
00:41:46,380 --> 00:41:47,920
But anyway, so
I'll talk about it.

453
00:41:47,920 --> 00:41:48,920
That's generalization.

454
00:41:48,920 --> 00:41:52,460
Like, if you're working
with general relativity,

455
00:41:52,460 --> 00:41:55,730
then you would actually not
quite work with this definition.

456
00:41:55,730 --> 00:41:58,990
You would work with some
generalization of this.

457
00:41:58,990 --> 00:42:02,770
But so what properties
should this distance have?

458
00:42:02,770 --> 00:42:05,430
It should have three properties.

459
00:42:05,430 --> 00:42:10,430
The first is that
for any x and y,

460
00:42:10,430 --> 00:42:12,910
the distance between
any two points

461
00:42:12,910 --> 00:42:16,310
should always give you
a non-negative number.

462
00:42:16,310 --> 00:42:18,870
So this here should
be non-negative.

463
00:42:18,870 --> 00:42:25,230
And if the distance
between two points is 0,

464
00:42:25,230 --> 00:42:31,750
then those two points
should be the same.

465
00:42:31,750 --> 00:42:36,030
So in fact, the distance
here is equal to 0

466
00:42:36,030 --> 00:42:39,150
if and only if x is equal to y.

467
00:42:41,950 --> 00:42:45,550
So that's the first property.

468
00:42:45,550 --> 00:42:49,230
And the second property is--

469
00:42:49,230 --> 00:42:52,010
just their distances are
non-negative, if you will.

470
00:42:52,010 --> 00:42:55,210
The second property is
that if you're looking at,

471
00:42:55,210 --> 00:42:57,770
what is the distance
from x to y?

472
00:42:57,770 --> 00:43:00,370
The distance from x to
y should be the same

473
00:43:00,370 --> 00:43:02,350
as the distance from y to x.

474
00:43:06,210 --> 00:43:07,190
So this is this.

475
00:43:07,190 --> 00:43:10,690
And this is sometimes referred
to that it's symmetric.

476
00:43:10,690 --> 00:43:12,730
It doesn't matter
whether you first--

477
00:43:12,730 --> 00:43:18,690
you begin at x and go to y or
you begin at y and go to x.

478
00:43:18,690 --> 00:43:21,010
And then the third
thing, which is

479
00:43:21,010 --> 00:43:24,030
kind of like the
nontrivial part of it

480
00:43:24,030 --> 00:43:26,330
and the thing that
makes it really useful,

481
00:43:26,330 --> 00:43:29,330
which is called the
triangle inequality--

482
00:43:29,330 --> 00:43:34,650
that if you have three points,
x, y, and z, in this space

483
00:43:34,650 --> 00:43:42,530
and you're looking at the
distance from x to z--

484
00:43:42,530 --> 00:43:46,010
so if you imagine that
you have three points--

485
00:43:46,010 --> 00:43:47,930
x, y.

486
00:43:47,930 --> 00:43:50,500
And then you have
another point, z.

487
00:43:50,500 --> 00:43:51,860
Sorry, no.

488
00:43:51,860 --> 00:43:53,100
That's not how I [INAUDIBLE].

489
00:43:53,100 --> 00:43:57,700
So you have three
points, x, y, and z.

490
00:43:57,700 --> 00:44:00,620
But you think about that
you're looking at the distance

491
00:44:00,620 --> 00:44:03,180
from x to z.

492
00:44:03,180 --> 00:44:06,680
Well, instead of going
directly from x to z,

493
00:44:06,680 --> 00:44:10,180
if you went over y,
then this distance

494
00:44:10,180 --> 00:44:13,380
here should be longer
than if you went directly.

495
00:44:13,380 --> 00:44:15,240
So this thing here
should be less than

496
00:44:15,240 --> 00:44:21,420
or equal to the distance from x
to y, plus the distance from y

497
00:44:21,420 --> 00:44:22,740
to z.

498
00:44:22,740 --> 00:44:27,540
And this here is the
nontrivial part of it.

499
00:44:27,540 --> 00:44:29,880
And this is called the
triangle inequality.

500
00:44:40,060 --> 00:44:42,513
Now, what are some
examples of this?

501
00:44:42,513 --> 00:44:43,930
Let's look at a
bunch of examples.

502
00:44:55,880 --> 00:45:03,640
The first example is
just where x is R.

503
00:45:03,640 --> 00:45:07,960
And so you have two
points in x in R.

504
00:45:07,960 --> 00:45:15,360
And now the distance between
them is simply x minus 1.

505
00:45:15,360 --> 00:45:17,880
This is obviously non-negative.

506
00:45:17,880 --> 00:45:21,480
If it's 0, that means
that x is equal to y.

507
00:45:21,480 --> 00:45:24,160
It's obviously also symmetric.

508
00:45:24,160 --> 00:45:26,600
And the triangle inequality
is this inequality

509
00:45:26,600 --> 00:45:28,040
that we're using all the time.

510
00:45:32,280 --> 00:45:39,520
Another example is if you're
looking at where x here

511
00:45:39,520 --> 00:45:41,800
is R squared, say.

512
00:45:41,800 --> 00:45:46,410
And then you're
thinking about x and y.

513
00:45:46,410 --> 00:45:47,885
You may think
about a mesh vector

514
00:45:47,885 --> 00:45:49,510
or whatever you want
to think about it.

515
00:45:49,510 --> 00:45:55,410
So this means that x here
has coordinates x1 and x2.

516
00:45:55,410 --> 00:45:59,580
And y has coordinates y1 and y2.

517
00:46:03,130 --> 00:46:09,360
And now the distance
between these two points

518
00:46:09,360 --> 00:46:17,930
is just the square root
of x1 minus y1 squared

519
00:46:17,930 --> 00:46:25,330
plus x2 minus y2 squared.

520
00:46:25,330 --> 00:46:26,990
That's the distance.

521
00:46:26,990 --> 00:46:31,040
And this here is often referred
to as the Euclidean distance.

522
00:46:33,850 --> 00:46:37,770
So this distance that you know.

523
00:46:37,770 --> 00:46:41,370
The distance is given
by Pythagoras' theorem.

524
00:46:41,370 --> 00:46:46,430
And so it's clear the
first two property,

525
00:46:46,430 --> 00:46:48,110
those are always clear.

526
00:46:48,110 --> 00:46:50,390
And the triangle
inequality, that's

527
00:46:50,390 --> 00:46:53,710
the one that sometimes
is tricky to prove.

528
00:46:53,710 --> 00:46:54,210
OK.

529
00:46:54,210 --> 00:46:57,310
So that's another
example of a distance.

530
00:46:57,310 --> 00:47:00,590
I want to give you
a few more examples.

531
00:47:00,590 --> 00:47:06,430
And so another example is
like where you do this in R3.

532
00:47:06,430 --> 00:47:10,730
And so you have, instead of
points having two coordinates,

533
00:47:10,730 --> 00:47:12,630
they now have three coordinates.

534
00:47:12,630 --> 00:47:14,590
And you do the sort
of same of thing.

535
00:47:14,590 --> 00:47:18,590
But now there would be a third
term under the square root sign,

536
00:47:18,590 --> 00:47:21,630
would be the square
of the difference

537
00:47:21,630 --> 00:47:23,410
between the third coordinates.

538
00:47:26,216 --> 00:47:27,870
OK.

539
00:47:27,870 --> 00:47:32,900
Then let me give you
a few more examples.

540
00:47:36,390 --> 00:47:41,150
There's one that is, to some
extent, simpler than this one

541
00:47:41,150 --> 00:47:43,240
here.

542
00:47:43,240 --> 00:47:45,820
And it's sometimes useful.

543
00:47:45,820 --> 00:47:55,080
But it's not as useful as
the Euclidean distances.

544
00:47:55,080 --> 00:47:58,820
And again, these sort of
things are generalized.

545
00:47:58,820 --> 00:48:01,640
They're an example of
something much more general.

546
00:48:01,640 --> 00:48:04,520
Like, this here is what's
called a Hilbert space,

547
00:48:04,520 --> 00:48:06,160
example of a Hilbert space.

548
00:48:06,160 --> 00:48:09,560
The next is something which
is an example of something

549
00:48:09,560 --> 00:48:10,900
called a Banach space.

550
00:48:10,900 --> 00:48:12,640
But so the next
example I will look at

551
00:48:12,640 --> 00:48:16,050
is what is sometimes
called the box distance.

552
00:48:20,640 --> 00:48:27,420
And so if space in
this case is R2--

553
00:48:27,420 --> 00:48:30,320
you could do it in
R3 also if you want.

554
00:48:30,320 --> 00:48:36,700
And you take two points
here, x and y in R2.

555
00:48:36,700 --> 00:48:43,700
And so this here, underline
x, has coordinates x1 and x2.

556
00:48:43,700 --> 00:48:49,180
And now the distance
between these two points

557
00:48:49,180 --> 00:48:54,880
would be-- you didn't do this
fancy thing with square root,

558
00:48:54,880 --> 00:48:59,340
but you did the more trivial
thing, where you're just adding.

559
00:48:59,340 --> 00:49:03,340
So you're taking two
points here in R2.

560
00:49:03,340 --> 00:49:06,060
This is maybe x1 comma x2.

561
00:49:06,060 --> 00:49:11,740
This is y1 So y2.

562
00:49:11,740 --> 00:49:15,260
The Euclidean distance is
simply this distance here.

563
00:49:15,260 --> 00:49:20,160
The box distance is where
you take this distance here

564
00:49:20,160 --> 00:49:22,860
and you're adding
this distance here.

565
00:49:22,860 --> 00:49:29,340
So this is this here
plus x2 minus y2.

566
00:49:29,340 --> 00:49:31,500
That's a box distance.

567
00:49:31,500 --> 00:49:33,640
And obviously, this
is a non-negative.

568
00:49:33,640 --> 00:49:36,380
It gives you-- for any
pair, it's non-negative.

569
00:49:36,380 --> 00:49:38,200
It's obviously symmetric.

570
00:49:38,200 --> 00:49:41,750
It doesn't matter the
ordering of these two.

571
00:49:41,750 --> 00:49:44,230
Its just switching the order
between absolute value sign

572
00:49:44,230 --> 00:49:46,390
anyway, so it doesn't matter.

573
00:49:46,390 --> 00:49:49,950
And it's also clear
that it's 0 if and only

574
00:49:49,950 --> 00:49:54,590
if these two points-- it
means that the coordinates are

575
00:49:54,590 --> 00:49:57,163
the same.

576
00:49:57,163 --> 00:50:00,670
And then, in this case,
the triangle inequality

577
00:50:00,670 --> 00:50:03,170
is not very hard to establish.

578
00:50:03,170 --> 00:50:05,630
Proving the triangle even
just for the Euclidean

579
00:50:05,630 --> 00:50:07,050
is a nontrivial thing.

580
00:50:10,550 --> 00:50:12,710
So that's another example.

581
00:50:12,710 --> 00:50:14,990
And I want to give you
a few more examples.

582
00:50:14,990 --> 00:50:19,790
Here's a sort of
interesting example.

583
00:50:24,950 --> 00:50:28,750
Suppose you take a interval--

584
00:50:28,750 --> 00:50:32,030
let's just make the
interval from 0 to 1.

585
00:50:32,030 --> 00:50:36,110
So suppose you're looking
at the interval from 0 to 1.

586
00:50:36,110 --> 00:50:42,170
And now looking at-- so this
here, this is now your space, x.

587
00:50:42,170 --> 00:50:52,970
And so by that, I mean
that x here consists of--

588
00:50:52,970 --> 00:50:57,110
so an element in x--

589
00:50:57,110 --> 00:50:58,290
let me write.

590
00:50:58,290 --> 00:50:58,920
An element.

591
00:51:01,770 --> 00:51:17,130
In x is a continuous function
on the interval from 0 to 1.

592
00:51:17,130 --> 00:51:20,690
So this means that if
you take two elements--

593
00:51:20,690 --> 00:51:25,170
so if you take one continuous
function on the interval

594
00:51:25,170 --> 00:51:29,170
from 0 to 1 and another
continuous function,

595
00:51:29,170 --> 00:51:31,290
what should be the
distance between two

596
00:51:31,290 --> 00:51:32,910
continuous functions?

597
00:51:35,650 --> 00:51:40,300
Well, the distance here-- so
this here is defined to be

598
00:51:40,300 --> 00:51:43,540
the maximum of--

599
00:51:43,540 --> 00:51:50,260
so if you're looking f
minus g, If f is continuous,

600
00:51:50,260 --> 00:51:52,580
g is continuous,
this is continuous.

601
00:51:56,460 --> 00:51:58,820
And now this is a
continuous function

602
00:51:58,820 --> 00:52:01,540
defined on this interval.

603
00:52:01,540 --> 00:52:08,060
So the extreme value theorem
say that the maximum in-- so

604
00:52:08,060 --> 00:52:09,860
this here is continuous.

605
00:52:09,860 --> 00:52:13,420
And in fact, even this
here is continuous.

606
00:52:13,420 --> 00:52:18,660
So the maximum of
this here is achieved.

607
00:52:18,660 --> 00:52:24,480
So this is the maximum,
where x is in 0 to 1.

608
00:52:24,480 --> 00:52:26,840
So this is achieved.

609
00:52:26,840 --> 00:52:30,180
And this is clearly,
again, symmetric.

610
00:52:30,180 --> 00:52:32,060
Because of the
absolute value sign,

611
00:52:32,060 --> 00:52:37,000
it's clearly symmetric,
in terms of f and g.

612
00:52:37,000 --> 00:52:39,920
It's also clear that
it's non-negative.

613
00:52:39,920 --> 00:52:43,040
And it's also clear
that it's 0 if

614
00:52:43,040 --> 00:52:46,680
and only if the two
functions are the same.

615
00:52:46,680 --> 00:52:48,720
And in this case,
it's quite easy

616
00:52:48,720 --> 00:52:56,760
to prove that this is a metric.

617
00:52:56,760 --> 00:53:01,500
OK, so maybe I'll
just leave that.

618
00:53:01,500 --> 00:53:02,000
OK.

619
00:53:02,000 --> 00:53:06,860
Now I want to give you a
couple of more examples.

620
00:53:06,860 --> 00:53:09,545
And then I'll start with
example and talk about--

621
00:53:09,545 --> 00:53:11,420
I want to give you a
couple of more examples.

622
00:53:11,420 --> 00:53:16,020
And the two next
examples, one is--

623
00:53:20,587 --> 00:53:22,920
and the reason why I want to
give you two other examples

624
00:53:22,920 --> 00:53:24,440
is that--

625
00:53:28,120 --> 00:53:31,600
so you know a lot of things
about distances already.

626
00:53:31,600 --> 00:53:34,130
And this gives you
excellent intuition

627
00:53:34,130 --> 00:53:36,850
to proving many things.

628
00:53:36,850 --> 00:53:40,210
But you should just keep
in mind that a metric space

629
00:53:40,210 --> 00:53:42,650
could be very wild.

630
00:53:42,650 --> 00:53:46,690
So I'll give you an example
of some strange and directly

631
00:53:46,690 --> 00:53:48,930
wild metric spaces.

632
00:53:48,930 --> 00:53:51,490
So the first one
is kind of strange.

633
00:53:51,490 --> 00:53:57,130
And then I'll give you another
example, which is wild.

634
00:53:57,130 --> 00:54:00,100
So the strange one, so
the strange example--

635
00:54:03,170 --> 00:54:08,230
and so this is like if you're
looking at the integers.

636
00:54:08,230 --> 00:54:09,920
So the space is
now the integers.

637
00:54:13,330 --> 00:54:16,450
And so you want to look
at, what is the distance

638
00:54:16,450 --> 00:54:20,423
between two integers?

639
00:54:20,423 --> 00:54:22,770
Now, of course,
your intuition would

640
00:54:22,770 --> 00:54:26,890
say that if one
integer is really large

641
00:54:26,890 --> 00:54:28,510
and the other one
is not very large,

642
00:54:28,510 --> 00:54:31,770
you would think that
the distance was large.

643
00:54:31,770 --> 00:54:34,070
But it doesn't have to be large.

644
00:54:34,070 --> 00:54:40,510
So I define this distance
to be 1/n minus 1/m.

645
00:54:43,150 --> 00:54:47,630
This here gives you so the
absolute value of the difference

646
00:54:47,630 --> 00:54:50,870
of 1 over each of them.

647
00:54:50,870 --> 00:54:53,830
This here gives you a distance.

648
00:54:53,830 --> 00:55:00,590
And in particular, you see
that if both n and m are

649
00:55:00,590 --> 00:55:04,790
really large, then
in this metric,

650
00:55:04,790 --> 00:55:06,390
they're very close together.

651
00:55:06,390 --> 00:55:10,310
It looks like the
distance is very small.

652
00:55:10,310 --> 00:55:16,110
Now, instead of actually proving
that this thing here is--

653
00:55:16,110 --> 00:55:18,150
so you could try to prove--

654
00:55:18,150 --> 00:55:22,110
so as usual, to
prove that this is

655
00:55:22,110 --> 00:55:24,930
a metric, the first
and second property,

656
00:55:24,930 --> 00:55:28,030
the property that is
non-negative, that's obvious.

657
00:55:28,030 --> 00:55:31,360
It's also obvious that
it's 0 if and only

658
00:55:31,360 --> 00:55:34,120
if n and m are the same.

659
00:55:34,120 --> 00:55:37,120
It's also obvious
that it's symmetric.

660
00:55:37,120 --> 00:55:39,700
The triangle inequality might
be not quite as obvious.

661
00:55:39,700 --> 00:55:41,600
It's not very hard in this case.

662
00:55:41,600 --> 00:55:46,080
But it actually is a triviality
because you could think

663
00:55:46,080 --> 00:55:50,240
about this one here as a map.

664
00:55:50,240 --> 00:55:58,400
Suppose you take a map from the
integers into the real numbers.

665
00:55:58,400 --> 00:56:00,840
And the map is just
that you take--

666
00:56:00,840 --> 00:56:06,320
the image of an integer
is just 1 over it.

667
00:56:06,320 --> 00:56:11,600
If you think about the
usual distance over here--

668
00:56:11,600 --> 00:56:13,380
and so the usual distance.

669
00:56:13,380 --> 00:56:17,560
By that, I mean that the
distance between x and y

670
00:56:17,560 --> 00:56:19,840
is just this.

671
00:56:19,840 --> 00:56:24,840
If you think about this, but
now thinking about that things

672
00:56:24,840 --> 00:56:27,360
here--

673
00:56:27,360 --> 00:56:30,820
and so you think about
the usual distance.

674
00:56:30,820 --> 00:56:36,900
But you're now restricted to
not just all real numbers,

675
00:56:36,900 --> 00:56:40,540
but just things that
are 1 over integers.

676
00:56:40,540 --> 00:56:45,740
On that space, it
gives you a metric.

677
00:56:45,740 --> 00:56:48,100
But then you can
just think about

678
00:56:48,100 --> 00:56:51,540
that this gives you another
way of labeling things.

679
00:56:51,540 --> 00:56:55,360
And so it's kind of a triviality
that it is a metric space.

680
00:56:55,360 --> 00:56:59,060
But distances looks a
little counterintuitive.

681
00:56:59,060 --> 00:57:04,100
Because if you have two very
large numbers, then actually,

682
00:57:04,100 --> 00:57:06,520
the distance between
them is small,

683
00:57:06,520 --> 00:57:10,860
even though those two large
numbers could be very far apart.

684
00:57:10,860 --> 00:57:14,820
So the last example I
want to give you, that's

685
00:57:14,820 --> 00:57:16,590
a example of a wild metric.

686
00:57:23,860 --> 00:57:26,880
This is one wild.

687
00:57:26,880 --> 00:57:31,450
And so, essentially, all
of these occur naturally.

688
00:57:36,550 --> 00:57:39,190
And so this example is what's
called the French railway

689
00:57:39,190 --> 00:57:45,630
metric, which is maybe not
a super flattering name

690
00:57:45,630 --> 00:57:46,850
but probably accurate.

691
00:57:46,850 --> 00:57:50,650
So the thing is that if
you travel in France--

692
00:57:50,650 --> 00:57:54,550
so if you imagine that
this is France here.

693
00:57:54,550 --> 00:57:57,030
Here, you have Paris.

694
00:57:57,030 --> 00:58:01,970
And if you want to go
to the French Riviera,

695
00:58:01,970 --> 00:58:04,030
which is maybe down here--

696
00:58:04,030 --> 00:58:07,030
so two cities on
the French Riviera

697
00:58:07,030 --> 00:58:13,590
that are incredibly close
together is Cannes and Nice.

698
00:58:13,590 --> 00:58:14,610
This is Cannes.

699
00:58:14,610 --> 00:58:16,150
And here, you have Nice.

700
00:58:16,150 --> 00:58:18,950
They're very, very
close together.

701
00:58:18,950 --> 00:58:22,630
But if you were going to
go with a French railroad,

702
00:58:22,630 --> 00:58:26,150
then you would have
to go to Paris first.

703
00:58:26,150 --> 00:58:29,330
So this here would take you
maybe 20 minutes by train

704
00:58:29,330 --> 00:58:30,930
if there was a train.

705
00:58:30,930 --> 00:58:32,950
But you have to
go to Paris first.

706
00:58:32,950 --> 00:58:35,510
And that's really far.

707
00:58:35,510 --> 00:58:38,330
The good thing is that the
French have this TGV, which

708
00:58:38,330 --> 00:58:41,730
is this incredible fast
train, but it still

709
00:58:41,730 --> 00:58:44,730
would take many hours.

710
00:58:44,730 --> 00:58:48,450
And so that's the idea of
the French railway network,

711
00:58:48,450 --> 00:58:53,810
that the space here is R2.

712
00:58:53,810 --> 00:58:57,890
And if you take two
points, x and y,

713
00:58:57,890 --> 00:59:08,210
in R2, then what is the
distance between this pair?

714
00:59:08,210 --> 00:59:10,700
So the distance
between the pair is--

715
00:59:19,290 --> 00:59:25,196
the distance
between x and y is--

716
00:59:33,860 --> 00:59:41,380
if x and y lie on the same-- so
imagine that this here is Paris.

717
00:59:41,380 --> 00:59:45,300
And so just think about
Paris as being the origin.

718
00:59:45,300 --> 00:59:49,100
So if x and y lie
on the same line--

719
00:59:49,100 --> 00:59:52,660
so lying on the same line
just means that x here

720
00:59:52,660 --> 00:59:54,980
is equal to some constant.

721
00:59:54,980 --> 01:00:01,340
If this here is y, then x
is like a constant times y.

722
01:00:01,340 --> 01:00:06,300
Or if one of them was 0,
then you could have that y

723
01:00:06,300 --> 01:00:09,580
is equal to a constant times x.

724
01:00:09,580 --> 01:00:16,680
So this just means that these
x and y lie on the same line.

725
01:00:16,680 --> 01:00:18,840
So if they lie on
the same line--

726
01:00:18,840 --> 01:00:25,920
so if x here is equal
to c times y or y

727
01:00:25,920 --> 01:00:37,880
is equal to c times x for some
real number, if that's the case,

728
01:00:37,880 --> 01:00:42,580
then it's just that the
distance between them,

729
01:00:42,580 --> 01:00:50,160
it's just like this usual
distance here between them.

730
01:00:50,160 --> 01:00:52,660
But if they don't lie
on the same line--

731
01:00:52,660 --> 01:00:58,000
they may lie on two lines that
are very, very close together--

732
01:00:58,000 --> 01:01:06,920
then the distance here would be
the sum of these two otherwise.

733
01:01:10,320 --> 01:01:10,820
OK.

734
01:01:10,820 --> 01:01:14,120
So that's the French
railway metric

735
01:01:14,120 --> 01:01:18,440
if I didn't have some sort of
strange properties, somewhat

736
01:01:18,440 --> 01:01:22,050
counterintuitive properties.

737
01:01:22,050 --> 01:01:25,250
The next thing I
want to talk about

738
01:01:25,250 --> 01:01:30,070
is convergence in
a metric space.

739
01:01:30,070 --> 01:01:33,730
So a lot of things we
have done generalizes

740
01:01:33,730 --> 01:01:37,770
to general metric spaces.

741
01:01:37,770 --> 01:01:41,960
So first, let's talk about, what
is a sequence in a metric space?

742
01:01:46,810 --> 01:01:50,570
So now you have a
metric space here.

743
01:01:50,570 --> 01:01:51,960
So this is a metric space.

744
01:01:56,810 --> 01:02:00,850
And a sequence xn is a sequence.

